From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] The MLD refresh created the station and left it an orphan

An MLO client with three setup links was reported to the controller with two.
Measured 2026-08-28 on the 8 GB agent, phone 6e:36:9a:de:cc:3c:

  wifi.apmld.ap-mld-1 stations   mlo_capable true, three mlo_links:
      link 0  f6:d9:a4:7d:d8:46  bssid 36:91:ac:f9:29:c6   2.4 GHz
      link 1  46:87:c2:bb:08:85  bssid 32:91:ac:f9:48:c6   5 GHz
      link 2  82:95:eb:7b:84:7c  bssid 3e:91:ac:f9:68:c6   6 GHz

  map.agent status               2.4 GHz sta f6:d9:...  is_affiliated_sta true
                                 5   GHz sta 46:87:...  is_affiliated_sta true
                                 6   GHz sta 82:95:...  is_affiliated_sta FALSE
                                                        mld_macaddr empty

  controller DB                  sta_mld.num_sta = 2, affiliated_sta 2 rows

The HAL had all three the whole time. The agent had all three as stations. Only
the third was never told which MLD it belongs to, so it was missing from
affiliated_sta[], from Num_AffiliatedSTA in the Associated STA MLD
Configuration Report, and from every row the controller writes from it.

The linkage has exactly one bootstrap: the association event, which calls
stamld_update() only when the event carries mlo_bssid, mlo_macaddr and
mlo_link_id together. A leg that appears any other way never gets it, and the
per-STA periodic path cannot repair it, because it looks the client up with

    wifi_get_mld_station(ap, s->mld_macaddr, s->macaddr, &mlsta)

and s->mld_macaddr is what stamld_update() would have set. A leg that missed
the bootstrap is looked up by a key it does not have, forever.

apmld_refresh_stations() is the one function positioned to repair it. It reads
wifi_get_mld_stations(), which returns the MLD address, the MLD bssid and every
link with its own address and link id - everything stamld_update() needs. It
used all of that to call wifi_add_sta() and nothing else. So the refresh was
creating precisely the orphan above: a station on the 6 GHz BSS, counted in
num_sta, belonging to no MLD.

It also only ever looked at sta[0]. Under an affiliated AP the entry that
matches ap->bssid is the link on that AP, which is sta[0] only when the client
associated there first. Walk num_link and match each one.

The guard is the same one the periodic path uses - mlo_capable, and an MLD
bssid that is not all zeros - so a client that is not MLO, or a reply that came
back half-filled, is added as a plain station exactly as before and nothing is
attached. mld_add_sta() is idempotent, so a leg already known costs a memcmp.

This is what the 6 GHz fronthaul AP section made reachable. Before b2df88a5e
the 6 GHz AP object did not exist, mld->affiliated_ap never named it, and this
loop could not visit that BSS at all. That fix closed the counting - num_sta
now sums to the number of links the radio holds, 4 == 4 on hardware this
morning - and this one closes the reporting.

--- a/src/mld.c	2026-08-28 08:50:37.878293437 +0200
+++ b/src/mld.c	2026-08-28 08:50:37.878362667 +0200
@@ -266,8 +266,42 @@
 			int j;
 
 			for (j = 0; j < num_mlsta; j++) {
-				if (!memcmp(mlsta[j].sta->bssid, ap->bssid, 6))
-					wifi_add_sta(a, ap->ifname, mlsta[j].sta->macaddr);
+				size_t k;
+
+				/* Every link, not sta[0]. The entry that matches
+				 * ap->bssid is the link sitting on this affiliated
+				 * AP, and that is sta[0] only when the client
+				 * happened to associate here first.
+				 */
+				for (k = 0; k < mlsta[j].num_link; k++) {
+					struct wifi_sta *link = &mlsta[j].sta[k];
+					struct sta *s;
+
+					if (memcmp(link->bssid, ap->bssid, 6))
+						continue;
+
+					s = wifi_add_sta(a, ap->ifname,
+							 link->macaddr);
+					if (!s)
+						continue;
+
+					/* Same guard as the per-STA periodic path:
+					 * a non-MLO client, or a reply that came
+					 * back half-filled, stays a plain station.
+					 */
+					if (!mlsta[j].mlo_capable ||
+					    hwaddr_is_zero(mlsta[j].bssid))
+						continue;
+
+					/* Without this the station exists, gets
+					 * counted in num_sta, and belongs to no
+					 * MLD - which is how a client with three
+					 * setup links was reported with two.
+					 */
+					stamld_update(a, ap, mlsta[j].macaddr,
+						      mlsta[j].bssid,
+						      link->mlo_link_id, s);
+				}
 			}
 		}
 	}
